            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Dogs symbolize uncleanness, moral impurity, and those outside the holy community. Depending on the context, they can represent generic wicked people, false teachers, apostates, vicious enemies, or specifically Gentiles (as outsiders to the covenant).\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Dogs symbolize the Gentile power—an unclean entity outside the covenant table that eventually crucified Christ and remains outside at the end.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Unclean, contemptible persons, wicked enemies, false teachers, or Gentiles.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The Gentile power.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book restricts the meaning of 'dogs' exclusively to the Gentile power, applying this specific identification to Psalm 22:16 (interpreting the dogs at the cross as Romans/Gentiles) and Revelation 22:15, whereas the consensus typically views the dogs in those latter passages as generic enemies or morally wicked individuals.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"There is no direct contradiction of the core meaning, as 'Gentiles' is a widely recognized referent in the consensus (especially for the Gospel accounts). The disagreement lies in the book elevating this specific referent to the universal meaning across all cited texts.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book selects 'Gentiles'—one of the primary referents recognized by the consensus—but narrows the symbol's overall scope by making 'the Gentile power' the exclusive meaning. It reclassifies passages normally read as describing generic persecutors or sinners as specifically referring to Gentiles.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"In Revelation 22:15, 'dogs' are listed alongside 'sorcerers, and whoremongers, and murderers, and idolaters,' which heavily implies a category of moral wickedness or false teaching rather than an ethnic or political designation like 'the Gentile power.'\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
